  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and warm conditions expected to continue \nthrough Saturday.\n\nSurface high pressure remains overhead through Friday. This \nwill continue to bring very dry conditions and calm winds. Highs\nwill be above average with temperatures in the 60s to even the \nlow 70s through Friday. Clear skies and light winds overnight \nyield favorable conditions for radiational cooling. Overnight \nlows will drop into the 30s tonight with lows Thursday and \nFriday night staying in the mid 30s to 40s. \n\nWhile remaining overhead, surface high pressure begins shifting\nnorthward on Saturday as a low pressure system develops off the\ncoast of Florida and tracks north. This results in increased\ncloud cover although dry conditions and calm winds persist.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Forecast remains uncertain as a coastal low tracks \nup the Eastern Seaboard Sunday into Monday. \n\nAn area of low pressure is forecast to develop off the Southeast \nU.S. coast late this week, then track northward along the coast this \nweekend. The low's initial forward speed is going to be slow as it \ngets trapped underneath a brief, weak upper ridge, then quickly \npicks up speed as it moves northeast with a strong upper trough \napproaching from the OH Valley. There remains considerable \ndifferences among the global guidance, both run to run and between \nmodels in each run. The ensemble guidance for both the European and \nGFS remain quite dispersive with low tracks well inland across the \nNC/VA Piedmont to well offshore of the Mid-Atlantic. Of particular \ninterest are the Euro and GFS AI models that are more in line with \neach other, both indicating a low track between I-95 and the \ncoastline. The low pressure quickly deepens and rapidly tracks \ntoward southern New England and Nova Scotia Monday into Monday \nnight. \n\nAt this time there remains too much uncertainty to pin down any \nspecific details or impacts to our area. Rain chances Sunday into \nSunday night have steadily increased, especially east of the Blue \nRidge with the highest certainty east of I-95. The exact track of \nthe low is going to be critical to where the heaviest rain falls, \nthough there could be a sharp gradient on the western side of the \nprecip shield from heavy to very light rain amounts. It is worth \nmentioning the higher-end of the ensemble spread indicates 2-4\" as a \nworst-case scenario, but many members also have little to no rain \nfor most of the area - this probably speak the best to how much \nuncertainty remains. The location of the heaviest QPF also varies \ngreatly from along US-15 to the Delmarva. Continue to monitor the \nforecast over the next few days as guidance hopefully starts to \nconverge on a solution for this weekend. \n\nA strong Canadian High builds across much of the central to eastern \nCONUS during the early part of next week. This is going to bring us \ndry, brisk, and much colder conditions. \n\nModels are indicating the potential for another system near \nThanksgiving, but even more spread exists with that system. \nMore updates as we get closer.",
